# Private members

Use TypeScript `private` and `private readonly` members. Do not use ECMAScript `#private` fields.

```ts
export class Gate {
	private closed = false;

	close(): void {
		if (this.closed) {
			return;
		}

		this.closed = true;
	}
}
```

When the class already has a public member of that name, the private member gets a `State` suffix. `#name` beside public `name` becomes `private nameState`.

A `#field in value` brand check has no TypeScript equivalent. Leave that field as `#private` and do not force the codemod through it.

`bun run check` fails while `#private` members remain. Update them with `bun run codemod`.
